# =============================================================================
# .env.example
#
# Committed TEMPLATE for the OPTIONAL `.env` file that `python-dotenv==1.2.2`
# loads at start-up. `.env` itself is untracked: `[.gitignore:L76]` ignores it
# with an exact, non-glob pattern, and that narrowness is deliberate - the
# pattern does NOT match this template, so no `!.env.example` negation exists
# and none must be added. A broader glob such as `.env*` would silently untrack
# this file and the whole configuration surface would vanish from the
# repository.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Then edit the copy. The copy is never committed; this template always is.
#
# EVERY KEY IS OPTIONAL, AND SO IS THE WHOLE FILE
#
# A fresh checkout has NEITHER `.env` NOR `configuration.properties`, so their
# absence is the normal case rather than an error. Every key below already has
# the same value hard-coded as a default in `app/config.py`; any single key may
# be deleted, and this whole file may be deleted outright - the Flask
# application still starts and the pytest harness still runs.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A SECRET HERE
#
# This template is tracked by git, so it must never contain a real credential,
# password, token, API key or private key - not even temporarily. `SECRET_KEY`
# in section 1 is an obvious placeholder and must stay one. Keep local secrets
# in your untracked `.env` or in the process environment. Secrets management and
# credential vaulting are out of scope for this project: templates document
# KEYS, never real VALUES.
#
# FILE FORMAT - dotenv
#
#   * `KEY=value`, one pair per line, UPPER_SNAKE_CASE keys.
#   * No `export ` prefix, and no spaces around `=`. `KEY = value` leaves a
#     leading space inside the value for any reader that does not strip it.
#   * NO VALUE IN THIS FILE IS QUOTED, on purpose - not even the two values that
#     contain spaces. A quote character is DATA to the Java `.properties` reader
#     behind `configuration.properties`, so quoting here would stop the two
#     templates from comparing byte for byte, and `EXPECTED_EMPTY_FIELD_MESSAGE`
#     in section 10 is an exact-match literal whose comparison must not be
#     disturbed. python-dotenv keeps everything up to end of line, so unquoted
#     values with spaces round-trip exactly.
#   * No trailing whitespace after a value: it would become part of the value
#     and would silently break an exact string comparison.
#   * Comments start with `#`. Every key below carries the file and line its
#     default came from, and names the preserved defect it controls where there
#     is one.
#   * UTF-8, LF line endings, pure ASCII, trailing newline.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE SITS IN THE PRECEDENCE CHAIN (AAP 0.3.1)
#
#     explicit constructor argument
#       -> environment variable
#         -> .env file                        <-- THIS FILE (third of five)
#           -> configuration.properties       (git-ignored runtime file)
#             -> hard-coded default in app/config.py
#
# Highest wins. A real environment variable - set in the shell, in the
# container, or by the CI job - OVERRIDES anything written here. Anything
# written here in turn OVERRIDES `configuration.properties`, which in turn
# overrides the hard-coded default in `app/config.py`. Only an explicit
# constructor argument outranks the environment. That ordering is why `.env`
# must be loaded with python-dotenv's default `override=False` behaviour: this
# file fills gaps, it never wins a fight with the real environment.
#
# SEE ALSO
#
#   configuration.properties.example  The same settings expressed in the Java
#                                     `.properties` format, one rung LOWER in
#                                     the chain. Every key it declares has a
#                                     counterpart here and the default values
#                                     agree, key for key; a divergence between
#                                     the two templates is a bug.
#   docs/configuration.md             The precedence chain and every setting's
#                                     source value.
#   docs/migration-parity.md          The authoritative D1-D9 defect register.
#
# EVERY VALUE BELOW IS THE SOURCE PROJECT'S OWN VALUE, cited inline against the
# file and line it was read from. Keys with no source construct are marked
# ADDITIVE and exist only because a deployable Flask application needs them.
# Values are data, not opportunities to modernise (AAP Rule T1). Several of them
# look wrong and ARE wrong: they are preserved deliberately (AAP Rule T4) and
# each one says so, because an uncommented `LogOut` or `true` is exactly the
# kind of thing a future maintainer "fixes", thereby breaking the parity
# criteria this port is accepted against.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Flask application - ADDITIVE
#
# The source system exposed no HTTP surface whatsoever: its orchestration was
# the Groovy scripted pipeline `node { ... }` [Jenkins:L1-L17] and its execution
# contract was the Surefire configuration [pom.xml:L17-L30]. The deliverable is
# defined as a Python 3 Flask application, so every setting in this section is
# ADDITIVE - required for the application to exist, ported from nothing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Configuration profile handed to the application factory `create_app(config_name)`.
# Accepted values: development | testing | production
# `development` suits a working copy. The container overrides it to `production`
# (Dockerfile ENV APP_CONFIG=production; docker-compose.yml passes
# ${APP_CONFIG:-production}), so this default never reaches a deployment.
APP_CONFIG=development

# Import path used by Flask's CLI for `flask run`, `flask shell` and
# `flask routes`. `wsgi.py` exposes the module-level `app` object that gunicorn
# serves as well (gunicorn.conf.py defaults `wsgi_app` to `wsgi:app`).
FLASK_APP=wsgi.py

# Debug mode is OFF and must STAY off in this template: Flask's debugger serves
# an interactive console to anyone who can reach the port, and a template is
# copied far more often than it is read. Enable it only in your untracked
# `.env`, and never in a shared environment.
FLASK_DEBUG=0

# Listen address of both the development server (`run.py`) and the production
# server (gunicorn.conf.py reads APP_HOST). 0.0.0.0 so a container or compose
# service is reachable from the host; narrow it to 127.0.0.1 if you only ever
# connect from the same machine.
APP_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Listen port, and the base value for the multi-instance offset in section 12:
# `make run`, `make serve` and gunicorn.conf.py all compute 8000 + CLONE_INDEX
# when APP_PORT is unset, so parallel clones of this repository do not collide.
# Setting it explicitly here PINS the port and defeats that shifting - comment
# this line out, or give each clone its own value, when several instances have
# to run side by side.
APP_PORT=8000

# Flask session-signing key. THIS IS A PLACEHOLDER, NOT A SECRET, and it is the
# one value in this file where a careless default becomes a real security
# problem. Generate your own into your untracked `.env` and never commit it:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
#
# docker-compose.yml falls back to this very same obvious placeholder, so the
# two agree; a real deployment must supply a real key through the environment.
SECRET_KEY=change-me-in-production

# Cross-origin policy for the report-retrieval endpoints, consumed by
# `flask-cors==6.0.5`. flask-cors reads `CORS_*` keys natively from the
# application config, and `app/extensions.py` deliberately constructs its
# extension singleton with no options so that this setting is not silently
# outranked by a constructor argument. `*` is the library's own default and is
# appropriate here because those endpoints are read-only, unauthenticated
# artifact downloads that carry no cookies: the four report artifacts of the
# documented runner's plugin list [README.md:L78-L81] plus the screen shots and
# error shots [README.md:L42-L43]. Narrow it to a comma-separated origin list
# before exposing the service on an untrusted network.
CORS_ORIGINS=*


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Production WSGI server (gunicorn) - ADDITIVE
#
# Flask's development server is not production grade, so the deliverable ships
# `gunicorn==26.0.0` plus `gunicorn.conf.py`. Every knob that file reads can be
# overridden here; only the ones with a STATIC default are set actively. The
# three that gunicorn.conf.py DERIVES or SIZES for itself are documented but
# left commented out, because writing them down as fixed values would replace a
# correct computed default with a stale literal.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# WSGI application to serve, `module:attribute`. Matches FLASK_APP above.
GUNICORN_WSGI_APP=wsgi:app

# Worker type. `sync` is correct for this service: its slow operations - the
# repository clone and the test run - are separate subprocesses with their own
# timeouts, not in-process I/O waits that an async worker could overlap.
GUNICORN_WORKER_CLASS=sync

# Request timeout in seconds. Generous because report generation and repository
# cloning are slow; the clone has its own CLONE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (section 3) and
# the test run its own TEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (section 4).
GUNICORN_TIMEOUT=120

# Socket to bind. DERIVED default: gunicorn.conf.py builds `APP_HOST:APP_PORT`,
# which is what keeps the CLONE_INDEX port shifting of section 12 working.
# Uncomment only to bind somewhere the two keys above cannot express, such as a
# UNIX socket.
# GUNICORN_BIND=0.0.0.0:8000

# Worker-process count. SIZED default: gunicorn.conf.py uses the conventional
# (2 x CPU) + 1, capped at 9 so a many-core CI host does not spawn dozens of
# workers for a small service. Left commented out on purpose - a fixed literal
# here would be wrong on every machine but one. Note this is the HTTP server's
# concurrency and has nothing to do with the ported test parallelism, which is
# PYTEST_WORKERS in section 4.
# GUNICORN_WORKERS=5

# Error-log verbosity. FALL-THROUGH default: gunicorn.conf.py reads this key,
# then LOG_LEVEL (section 11), then `info`, and lower-cases the result - so
# leaving this unset makes the server track the application's own log level
# instead of drifting away from it.
# GUNICORN_LOG_LEVEL=info

# gunicorn.conf.py accepts these further overrides. They tune the HTTP server
# only - none of them affects a ported behaviour, a report artifact or a parity
# criterion - so they are listed with their defaults instead of being repeated as
# active keys, and the configuration file stays the single place they are read:
#
#   GUNICORN_BACKLOG=2048              pending-connection queue depth
#   GUNICORN_THREADS=1                 threads per sync worker
#   GUNICORN_GRACEFUL_TIMEOUT=30       seconds allowed for a clean shutdown
#   GUNICORN_KEEPALIVE=5               seconds to hold an idle connection
#   GUNICORN_MAX_REQUESTS=0            0 disables worker recycling
#   GUNICORN_MAX_REQUESTS_JITTER=0     jitter added to the recycle threshold
#   GUNICORN_PRELOAD=false             must stay false: the factory reads
#                                      configuration at import time, and
#                                      preloading would share that state across
#                                      every worker
#   GUNICORN_CHDIR=.                   working directory, kept at the repository
#                                      root so `target/` resolves
#   GUNICORN_ACCESS_LOG=-              `-` means stdout, so containers and CI
#   GUNICORN_ERROR_LOG=-               capture both streams
#   GUNICORN_PROC_NAME=testinium-qa    matches the project name [pom.xml:L8]


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Stage 'Clone code' - port of [Jenkins:L2-L4]
#
# The Groovy `git '<url>'` step is not a bare clone: it also handles workspace
# cleanup, credentials and source-control polling. `app/services/clone_service.py`
# replaces it with an explicit argument-list subprocess call - never a shell
# string, so a URL can never be interpolated into a command - that handles the
# already-cloned case by fetching and resetting instead of failing, and that
# always runs under a timeout.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D7 - two contradictory repository URLs.
# The pipeline clones https://github.com/BalamiRR/Upgenix-QA.git [Jenkins:L3],
# while the README's "Framework set up" section instructs cloning
# https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git [README.md:L59].
# Per AAP AMB-7 the PIPELINE value is the runtime default: executable
# configuration outranks prose when the two disagree, and the Jira key prefix
# `UPGN` [README.md:L115] agrees with the pipeline URL. Both strings are
# retained rather than silently unified, so no information is lost.
CLONE_URL=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Upgenix-QA.git

# The README's URL, offered here as a commented-out ALTERNATIVE. Uncomment this
# line and comment the one above to clone the repository the README documents.
# Mirrors the same commented alternative in configuration.properties.example.
# CLONE_URL=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git

# The README's URL also kept as a value in its own right, so the discrepancy
# stays machine-readable and the configuration endpoint can report both.
# Source: [README.md:L59]. Counterpart of clone.url.documented.
CLONE_URL_DOCUMENTED=https://github.com/BalamiRR/Testinium-QA.git

# The pipeline's `git '<url>'` step [Jenkins:L3] passes no explicit ref, so it
# checks out the repository's default branch.
CLONE_BRANCH=main

# ADDITIVE. Working-copy destination, relative to the repository root: the
# Groovy step wrote into the Jenkins workspace, which has no analogue in a plain
# Python process.
CLONE_DIR=workspace

# ADDITIVE but REQUIRED, in seconds. A clone that blocks for ever would hang the
# whole ported pipeline, so the subprocess call is always given an explicit
# timeout.
CLONE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Stage 'Run tests' - port of [Jenkins:L6-L11] and of the Maven Surefire
#    configuration [pom.xml:L21-L29]
#
# `app/services/test_runner_service.py` dispatches the platform-appropriate
# command - the port of `if(isUnix()){ sh } else { bat }` [Jenkins:L7-L11] - and
# maps pytest's exit code onto a stage verdict. `pytest.ini` carries the same
# selection and reporting options for a direct `pytest` invocation.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D2 - THE DEFAULT TAG EXPRESSION SELECTS ZERO SCENARIOS.
# Faithful port of the documented runner's tags = "@LogOut" [README.md:L87].
# pytest-bdd turns every Gherkin tag into a pytest mark and strips the leading
# `@`, which is why the value carries no `@`; `pytest.ini` spells the same
# selection as `-m "LogOut"`.
# NO SCENARIO CARRIES THAT TAG. The feature file's only tags are @Login,
# @UPGN-286, @UPGN-287, @UPGN-288, @SalesManager and @PosManager
# [README.md:L104-L148], so a default run reports "6 deselected, 0 selected" and
# pytest exits with code 5, "no tests ran".
# EXIT CODE 5 MUST BE REPORTED AS SUCCESS - a successful zero-scenario run
# (validation criterion V6). The source pipeline went green in exactly this
# situation, because Surefire simply found nothing matching the tag filter and
# the publisher's all-`-1` thresholds gated nothing; mapping 5 to failure would
# make the port fail where the original succeeded. `make test` implements that
# mapping explicitly.
# Set this key to an EMPTY value (`TAG_EXPRESSION=`) to run the whole suite.
TAG_EXPRESSION=LogOut

# PRESERVED DEFECT D3 - THE BUILD CAN NEVER FAIL.
# Direct port of <testFailureIgnore>true</testFailureIgnore> [pom.xml:L25].
# While this is true the ported runner treats pytest's exit codes as:
#   0 -> success               all selected scenarios passed
#   1 -> success, NON-GATING   scenarios ran and some failed
#   5 -> success, zero scenarios  everything deselected (D2 above)
#   2 -> hard failure          execution interrupted
#   3 -> hard failure          internal error
#   4 -> hard failure          usage / command-line error
# The six report thresholds in section 5 are all -1, which the publisher treats
# as "no threshold", so they gate nothing either; and report generation runs
# even after a FAILED test stage because `app/services/pipeline_service.py`
# invokes it from a finally-equivalent path (validation criterion V12).
# Set to false to make test failures gate the pipeline - a deliberate deviation
# from source behaviour; record it in docs/migration-parity.md if you do.
IGNORE_TEST_FAILURES=true

# Port of <parallel>methods</parallel> plus
# <useUnlimitedThreads>true</useUnlimitedThreads> [pom.xml:L22-L23] onto
# pytest-xdist worker allocation. `logical` lets xdist size the pool from the
# machine, which is the closest available analogue of "unlimited threads".
# Parallel execution does NOT compromise report fidelity: a parallel run emits a
# complete target/cucumber.json containing every scenario.
PYTEST_WORKERS=logical

# PRESERVED-BUT-DISABLED tuning default. pom.xml keeps its thread count
# commented out - `<!--                    <threadCount>4</threadCount>-->`
# [pom.xml:L24] - and so does this template, exactly (AAP O7). The active
# default above stays `logical`; uncomment the line below to pin four workers.
# PYTEST_WORKERS=4

# ADDITIVE guard rail, in seconds, so a hung browser session cannot stall a run
# for ever. Applies to the whole pytest invocation, not to a single scenario.
TEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1800

# pytest-bdd's Gherkin terminal reporter. OFF by default, and that is a HARD
# constraint rather than a preference: the reporter cannot coexist with
# pytest-xdist - it raises an explicit exception during configuration and aborts
# the run with an internal error - and the default invocation is parallel
# because parallelism is itself a parity requirement [pom.xml:L22-L23].
# `make test-pretty` is the serial escape hatch: it runs `-n 0` (xdist loaded but
# distribution off) together with `--gherkin-terminal-reporter`, which is the
# only combination that satisfies both plugins (validation criterion V8).
# The PrettyReports analogue of [README.md:L78-L81] does NOT depend on this
# switch: `app/reporting/pretty_reports.py` post-processes target/cucumber.json,
# exactly as the Java reporting plugin [pom.xml:L66-L70] rendered its directory
# from the JSON rather than from terminal output.
GHERKIN_TERMINAL_REPORTER=false


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Stage 'Generate report' - port of [Jenkins:L13-L15]
#
# Every value in this section is read straight off the `cucumber` publisher
# invocation [Jenkins:L15], which the updated pipeline preserves
# byte-identically. `app/reporting/thresholds.py` freezes the same constants in
# Python under the same names, and parity criterion V4 asserts they still match.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D3, continued - all six thresholds are -1, which the
# publisher treats as "no threshold". Nothing here can ever fail a build.
REPORT_FAILED_FEATURES_NUMBER=-1
REPORT_FAILED_SCENARIOS_NUMBER=-1
REPORT_FAILED_STEPS_NUMBER=-1
REPORT_PENDING_STEPS_NUMBER=-1
REPORT_SKIPPED_STEPS_NUMBER=-1
REPORT_UNDEFINED_STEPS_NUMBER=-1

# sortingMethod: 'ALPHABETICAL' [Jenkins:L15]. A no-op while the project has
# exactly one feature, but implemented anyway so the behaviour survives the
# addition of further features instead of becoming a latent divergence.
REPORT_SORTING_METHOD=ALPHABETICAL

# fileIncludePattern: '**/*.json' [Jenkins:L15], preserved VERBATIM AS DATA.
# This is the publisher's own glob, not a transformation pattern: never rewrite
# it. It is also the reason the artifact root in section 6 keeps its
# Java-flavoured name - leaving the reports under `target/` means this contract
# needs no edit at all.
REPORT_FILE_INCLUDE_PATTERN=**/*.json


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Artifact layout - the four report artifacts declared by the documented
#    runner's plugin list [README.md:L78-L81], plus the shots directories
#    [README.md:L42-L43] and the retained Surefire report directory
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Artifact root, as the bare directory NAME so that it compares equal, byte for
# byte, to app/utils/paths.py::TARGET_DIR_NAME, to `TARGET_DIR ?= target` in the
# Makefile and to `target.dir` in configuration.properties.example. DO NOT
# rename this directory: the publisher glob in section 5 depends on it, and
# renaming it is precisely the change that would break the CI contract this
# migration went out of its way to keep.
TARGET_DIR=target

# json:target/cucumber.json - the one artifact the publisher glob matches. Its
# writer does NOT create this parent directory, so the `target/` tree must exist
# BEFORE a run; app/utils/paths.py, the Makefile `test` target and
# tests/conftest.py each guarantee that independently (criterion V7).
CUCUMBER_JSON_PATH=target/cucumber.json

# html:target/cucumber-reports.html - served as a static artifact and never
# re-rendered, so it cannot diverge from what the source toolchain produced.
CUCUMBER_HTML_PATH=target/cucumber-reports.html

# rerun:target/rerun.txt - one `<feature-uri>:<scenario-line>` entry per failing
# scenario, derived deterministically from the JSON report so the manifest can
# never drift away from it (criterion V9).
RERUN_TXT_PATH=target/rerun.txt

# me.jvt.cucumber.report.PrettyReports:target/cucumber - produced by
# post-processing target/cucumber.json, exactly as the Java reporting plugin
# [pom.xml:L66-L70] rendered its directory from the JSON.
PRETTY_REPORTS_DIR=target/cucumber

# Screen shots and error shots [README.md:L42-L43]. Both sit under the artifact
# root so `make clean`, the port of `mvn clean`, wipes them with everything else.
SCREENSHOTS_DIR=target/screenshots
ERROR_SHOTS_DIR=target/error-shots

# Surefire's report directory name is retained for report-consumer parity, so
# any downstream consumer keyed on this path keeps finding content. It is
# populated by the JUnit-XML output the runner requests
# (target/surefire-reports/TEST-CukesRunner.xml).
SUREFIRE_REPORTS_DIR=target/surefire-reports


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Screen shots and error shots [README.md:L42-L43]
#
# The README states the project generates screen shots for your tests "if you
# enable it", and generates error shots for failed test cases as well. The two
# defaults below follow that wording literally.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Opt-in, exactly as "if you enable it" describes: shots of passing steps are
# off by default.
SCREENSHOTS_ENABLED=false

# Error shots for failed test cases are described unconditionally, so they are
# on by default. Captured by the screenshot-on-failure hook in
# tests/step_defs/conftest.py.
ERROR_SHOTS_ENABLED=true


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Location of the optional runtime properties file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Path to the git-ignored `configuration.properties` [.gitignore:L3] - the
# fourth rung of the precedence chain, one below this file. It cannot be set
# inside that file (a setting cannot locate its own file), so this is an
# environment-only override. The file is optional: `app/utils/properties.py`
# degrades silently when it is absent, unreadable or malformed, which is what
# makes a fresh checkout work with no configuration at all. The committed
# template is `configuration.properties.example`.
CONFIGURATION_PROPERTIES_PATH=configuration.properties



# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Browser automation - Selenium WebDriver
#
# The source project's prerequisite list asked for a browser driver with its
# class path already set [README.md:L53]; the keys below replace that manual step
# with configuration. `tests/support/driver_factory.py` builds the driver and
# `tests/pages/*` hold every locator, so this is the whole surface a browser
# change touches.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The browser to drive. Deliberately SINGLE-VALUED: a cross-browser and cross-OS
# test matrix is out of scope, so this is one browser and not a browser list.
BROWSER=chrome

# ADDITIVE. Headless by default because the build environment has no display.
HEADLESS=true

# Driver provisioning strategy. Selenium 4 resolves drivers itself through
# Selenium Manager; the `webdriver-manager` distribution is retained as the
# one-to-one counterpart of io.github.bonigarcia:webdrivermanager
# [pom.xml:L42-L46]. Accepted values: selenium-manager | webdriver-manager
DRIVER_MANAGER=selenium-manager

# ADDITIVE wait budgets, in seconds. tests/pages/base_page.py uses EXPLICIT
# waits; the implicit wait is kept only as a backstop, and mixing the two
# aggressively is why the explicit budget is the larger of the pair.
IMPLICIT_WAIT_SECONDS=10
EXPLICIT_WAIT_SECONDS=30
PAGE_LOAD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# Base URL of the EXTERNAL application under test - the French-language site the
# login scenarios drive. Deliberately documented with an EMPTY value and left
# COMMENTED OUT: no address for it exists anywhere in the source project, and
# inventing one is forbidden (AAP Rule T6). The only two URLs in evidence are the
# two git remotes in section 3, and neither is the application under test. That
# application is external, unmodifiable and unreachable from CI, so the
# browser-driven scenarios cannot run end to end here; the limitation is recorded
# as risk R6 rather than papered over. Uncomment and supply your own
# environment's address to run them locally.
# Mirrors `#base.url=` in configuration.properties.example.
# BASE_URL=

# Address of a remote Selenium Grid / standalone container, consumed by the
# optional `selenium` compose profile (docker-compose.yml:L51). EMPTY means "no
# remote": the driver factory starts a local browser through the strategy chosen
# above. With `docker compose --profile selenium up -d` the in-network address is
# http://selenium:4444 and the host-side address is http://localhost:$SELENIUM_PORT
# (section 12). This is Selenium infrastructure, not the application under test.
SELENIUM_REMOTE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Behaviour-parity literals
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PRESERVED DEFECT D5 - the localisation and the comment disagree.
# The feature file's explanatory comment says the expected text is
# "Please fill out this field" [README.md:L130], while the ASSERTION five lines
# later expects the French "Veuillez renseigner ce champ." [README.md:L135].
# The assertion is the executable truth, so the French string is the value here.
# It is 29 bytes and pure ASCII: French-language, but not multi-byte. Compare it
# EXACTLY - do not translate it to match the English comment, do not drop the
# trailing period, and do not normalise or strip it in any way. Unquoted on
# purpose (see the file-format notes at the top): a quote would become part of
# the value in the properties template and the two would stop agreeing.
EXPECTED_EMPTY_FIELD_MESSAGE=Veuillez renseigner ce champ.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 11. Logging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Standard-library level name: DEBUG | INFO | WARNING | ERROR | CRITICAL.
# `app/logging_config.py` configures structured logging - the application never
# prints. gunicorn falls back to this value for its own error log (section 2).
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# Destination for the structured log. The `.log` suffix is deliberate: the
# repository has ignored `*.log` since before the migration [.gitignore:L6], so
# this path stays untracked without any new ignore rule being needed.
LOG_FILE=logs/testinium-qa.log


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 12. Multi-instance and container settings - ADDITIVE
#
# Nothing in the source system had to coexist with a second copy of itself; a
# Jenkins workspace was exclusive. These keys let several clones of this
# repository run their own instance side by side without colliding on a
# host-global resource.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Offset applied to host-global resources: the listen port becomes
# 8000 + CLONE_INDEX (Makefile, gunicorn.conf.py) and the compose project name
# becomes testinium-qa-$CLONE_INDEX (docker-compose.yml:L15). Environment-only by
# design, which is why it has no counterpart in configuration.properties.example.
# Remember that an explicit APP_PORT in section 1 outranks this offset.
CLONE_INDEX=0

# Host port published for the optional `selenium` compose service
# (docker-compose.yml:L73). Only relevant with `--profile selenium`.
SELENIUM_PORT=4444

# Concurrent browser sessions the standalone Selenium container will accept
# (docker-compose.yml:L75). Sized to match a parallel run rather than a serial
# one, because the default invocation is parallel [pom.xml:L22-L23].
SE_NODE_MAX_SESSIONS=4


# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE
#
#   A real secret of any kind
#       No credential, password, token, API key or private key belongs in a
#       tracked template. SECRET_KEY carries an obvious placeholder and nothing
#       else here is sensitive. Secrets management and credential vaulting are
#       out of scope: templates document KEYS, never real VALUES.
#   Test account credentials
#       The only account data in evidence lives in the feature file's Examples
#       tables [README.md:L137-L148] and stays there. It is test data for the
#       ported scenarios, not configuration, and it is deliberately not
#       duplicated here.
#   An address for the application under test
#       See BASE_URL in section 9: none exists in the source project and
#       inventing one is forbidden (AAP Rule T6).
#   Database, cache, message-broker or task-queue settings
#       This system has no datastore, no cache, no broker and no queue. The
#       rewrite mandate is that no feature is dropped and none is introduced.
#   NODE_ENV, JAVA_HOME, MAVEN_OPTS or any other Node.js / JVM variable
#       The repository has never contained Node.js code, and the Java toolchain
#       is not required to run anything here: `pom.xml` is a read-only parity
#       contract that is never compiled.
#   Cross-browser / cross-OS matrix keys
#       Out of scope. BROWSER names ONE browser, and
#       `app/utils/platform_exec.py` preserves the pipeline's isUnix() branch
#       [Jenkins:L7-L11] and nothing more.
#   Keys for the three unexercised user roles
#       Out of scope. Only PosManager and SalesManager appear in the
#       specification [README.md:L106-L109]; the remaining three of five roles
#       are named nowhere and are not invented here.
#   A renamed artifact root
#       TARGET_DIR is `target` and stays `target`. See section 5.
# =============================================================================
